  booktitle={2025 28th International Conference on Computer and Information Technology (ICCIT)}, 
  title={A Reproducible Benchmark for Prompt Injection Vulnerability Assessment in Small-To-Medium Language Models}, 
  year={2025},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={1-6},
  abstract={This paper presents a reproducible, automated benchmarking infrastructure to automatically evaluate promptinjection and jailbreak vulnerabilities across small- to mediumscale language models. Our assessment uses a dynamic set of 225 adversarial prompts, sourced from 75 baselines across AdvBench, HarmfulQA, and JailbreakBench, resulting in 2,250 model-prompt interactions across ten Ollama-hosted systems. Model output is evaluated according to a hybrid dual-judge approach-an LLM-based judge with phi-3 fallback-aidsed by keyword-driven heuristics, the results being analyzed under strict statistical controls (Welch's t-tests adjusted for Bonferroni multiple-comparison penalty). Empirical results report a mean Attack Success Rate (ASR) of 65.2 %, albeit significant variance across models $(0.44 \%-97.3 \%$; e.g., TinyLlama, 97.3 %, SmolLM-135M, 93.8%, LLaMA-3, 0.44%). Additional analysis reveals variance between attack typologies and prompt sources, authority-based attacks achieving an ASR, on average, of 69.1 %, and JailbreakBench prompts the most effective overall (74.4 %). Collectively, these results demonstrate wide disparities in model robustness, establish principled methodology for vulnerability evaluation, and provide actionable guidance toward mitigating adversarial exploitation of language models.},
  keywords={Payloads;Radio broadcasting;Frequency modulation;Filtering;Filters;Circuits and systems;Radio access networks;Regional area networks;Protocols;HTTP;Prompt injection;Jailbreaking;Language models;Adversarial attacks;Model vulnerability;Machine learning security;Statistical analysis;Ollama;Automated benchmarking},
